Every founder we talk to wants AI in their product. Far fewer know *when* to bring the first dedicated AI engineer on board. Hire too early and you pay a senior salary for problems that a good API call would solve. Hire too late and you fall behind competitors who already turned data into a moat.

After placing AI and data talent across LATAM, the US, and Europe, we've noticed the same five signals in companies that got the timing right.

## 1. You have data, and it's already a headache

If your team is arguing about where the data lives, how clean it is, or who owns it, that friction is a signal. An AI engineer turns that mess into leverage.

## 2. Off-the-shelf models stopped being enough

Wrapping a foundation model is a great start. But once your differentiation depends on *how* you use it — retrieval, evaluation, fine-tuning, guardrails — you need someone who owns that layer.

## 3. Someone is doing AI work by accident

There's usually a backend engineer or data analyst quietly holding the AI pipeline together on nights and weekends. That's your signal — and often the person who should help define the role.

> The best first AI hire rarely comes from a job board. They come from a warm, precise search.

## 4. The roadmap has AI on the critical path

When a launch date depends on an AI feature shipping, the work is no longer experimental. It needs an owner who is accountable for it.

## 5. You can describe success in one sentence

"Cut support resolution time in half." "Rank listings better than the incumbent." If you can name the outcome, you can hire for it. If you can't, hire clarity before you hire an engineer.

